<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deir al-Qamar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Deir el Qamar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Deir al-Qamar is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chouf">Chouf mountains</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>. It was the seat of the Ma'n and Shihab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a> until Bashir II moved his court to nearby Beiteddine, and it preserves the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fakhr-al-din-ii">Fakhr al-Din II</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> and Christian population suffered a massacre in the civil war of 1860, and many of its old stone houses and palaces survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deir-al-qamar.jpg" alt="Deir al-Qamar" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Patrice Bon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deir_El-Qamar_Minist%C3%A8re_du_tourisme.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Deir al-Qamar, Arabic for Monastery of the Moon, is a town south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> in the Chouf District of the Mount Lebanon Governorate. It lies five kilometres outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beiteddine-palace">Beit ed-Dine</a> at an average altitude of 800 metres. Its oldest written reference, from 1257 and 1261, is in deeds by which Julian of Shouf and Andrew of Chouf sold their lands and villages in the lordship of Chouf to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the 16th to the 18th century Deir al-Qamar was the capital and residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a>, and it was a centre of Lebanese literary tradition and an important commercial and artisanal centre for the villages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chouf">Shuf</a>, with textile production, metalworking and soap manufacture.</p><p>In 1841 the town became a major site of conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> notables and its Maronite inhabitants, over land, taxation and the restoration of the notables' authority after the end of Egyptian rule. After the conflicts of the 1840s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a> was divided into Christian and Druze districts, and Deir al-Qamar became the winter capital of the southern Druze district.</p><p>In 1860, during the civil war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> and Christians, the town was destroyed and set ablaze, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> sent a French contingent to rebuild it. In 1864 it elected the first municipality in the Arab provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. A Jewish community lived in the town, with a synagogue built in 1638 and a beth din. After the 1837 earthquake at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safed">Safed</a>, thirty-five armed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from Deir al-Qamar went to help clear the debris.</p><p>In 1847 nine local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were falsely accused of murdering a Maronite child who had died of exposure, and were released with the help of the British consul. The civil conflict of 1860 ended the community: the Jews, like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a>, were forced to leave, and sold their homes and lands to Christians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town keeps its stone houses with red tile roofs, and it was placed under monument protection in 1945. Its buildings include the Fakhreddine Mosque, built in 1493 by the Ma'nid emir Fakhr al-Din I and the oldest mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a>, the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fakhr-al-din-ii">Fakhr al-Din II</a>, and the palace of the Emir Yusuf Shihab, which houses the Municipal Council.</p><p>The Maronite church of Our Lady of the Hill, Saydet el Talle, is said to have been revered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> as well as Christians, who called it the Church of the Virgin of the Druze. Most inhabitants are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maronites">Maronites</a>, with a Melkite minority.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Deir al-Qamar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The emirs ruled a feudal society of Druze and Maronite lords and peasants from <b>Deir al-Qamar</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/mount-lebanon-emirate">Mount Lebanon Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
